          "type": "thinking",
          "thinking": "Now I have a good understanding of the codebase. Let me analyze what needs to be done:\n\n1. Add a function `_orphan_entries_problem(orphan: dict, pred: dict) -> str | None` to `record/record.py`\n2. The function should:\n   - Take `orphan[\"body\"][\"entries_b64\"]` - a list of base64-encoded canonical entry bytes\n   - Return `None` when the decoded entries exactly match the attested range:\n     - Count must be `to_seq - from_seq + 1`\n     - First entry's `prev` must chain from `pred[\"prev_sha256\"]`\n     - Each subsequent entry must chain from the previous one's sha256\n     - Last entry's sha256 must equal `pred[\"head_sha256\"]`\n   - Return a problem string:\n     - Containing \"range\" when count is wrong\n     - Containing \"chain\" when an entry's `prev` doesn't link\n     - Containing \"head\" when the last hash doesn't match `head_sha256`\n3. Wire it into wherever orphans are checked (in `cmd_monitor`)\n4. All existing tests must pass\n\nLet me look at the orphan structure more carefully. In `check_body`, an orphan has:\n- `by`\n- `log_index`\n- `reason`\n- `statement_b64`\n\nBut the task says `orphan[\"body\"][\"entries_b64\"]` is a list of base64-encoded canonical entry bytes. So I need to add this field to the orphan body validation and then use it in the monitor.\n\nLooking at `cmd_monitor`, it currently checks:\n1. The orphan's statement matches Rekor's payload hash\n2. The attested head wasn't published and later removed\n\nBut it doesn't check that the orphan actually contains the entries it claims to attest. That's what we need to add.\n\nLet me first add the `entries_b64` field to the orphan body validation, then add the `_orphan_entries_problem` function, and then wire it into `cmd_monitor`.\n\nLet me start by modifying the code.",
